// @korulang/unikraft-sched — Unikraft's scheduler (`uksched`), lifted.
//
//   import unikraft/sched
//
// WHAT THIS IS. `lib/uksched` is 42 exported functions over a thread lifetime
// that Unikraft has drawn as an ASCII diagram in its own header
// (`include/uk/thread.h:123-161`) and then encoded 112 times as `UK_ASSERT`.
// `UK_ASSERT` expands to `do {} while(0)` whenever `CONFIG_LIBUKDEBUG_ENABLE_ASSERT`
// is off — which is every image anyone ships. This module transcribes the part
// of that ordering a CALLER can violate into phantom states, so the compiler
// refuses those programs and the emitted code carries no check at all.
//
// WHY THIS IS A DIFFERENT SHAPE FROM `unikraft/blk`. A block device is one
// object moving forward through a bring-up. A thread is two things at once:
// an ORDERING (you may not set an entry point twice; you may not attach a
// thread that is already attached) and a LIFETIME that the thread itself can
// end. `ukblkdev` has nothing that corresponds to "the resource ran". This
// module's asymmetry gate is exactly that: a thread that was allocated a stack,
// a TLS and an aux stack, attached to the scheduler, and then destroyed WITHOUT
// EVER BEING GIVEN CPU. On a 2 MB unikernel a default thread stack is 256 KiB —
// an eighth of the machine, taken and returned with no instruction executed.
//
// THE RATCHET. One handle, six states, one direction:
//
//     reserve     mints  <container!>                  uk_thread_create_container
//     prime       needs  <!container>  mints <primed!>   uk_thread_container_init_fn1
//     attach      needs  <!primed>     mints <queued!>    uk_sched_thread_add
//     run         needs  <!queued|!ran> mints <ran!>      sched_yield  (+ proof it ran)
//     join        needs  <!queued|!ran> mints <ran!>      sched_yield until finished
//     park        needs  <!ran>        mints <parked!>    uk_thread_block
//     unpark      needs  <!parked>     mints <ran!>       uk_thread_wake
//     terminate   needs  <!ran|!parked>                   uk_sched_thread_terminate
//     never-ran   needs  <!queued>     mints <ran!>       NO C CALL — the escape
//     unspawned   needs  <!container|!primed> mints <detached!>  NO C CALL
//     release     needs  <!detached>                      uk_thread_release
//
// THE ASYMMETRY (pillar 4). `terminate` accepts ONLY <!ran> or <!parked>, and
// <ran!> is minted only by `run`, `join`, `unpark` or the named escape. So
//
//     attach(t) | ok q |> terminate(thread: q)
//
// does not compile. This is the thread twin of gzip's `fed` gate
// (koru-libs/gzip/index.kz:258, where `finish` accepts only <!fed> and only
// `push` mints it) and of the transaction showcase's rule that `close` takes
// <!active> and not <!connected> (koru tests/.../2104_14_open_tx_commit_close/
// db.kz). RAII guarantees a thread is REAPED; it cannot force it to be RUN.
//
// The escape is deliberate, named and greppable: `sched:never-ran`. A program
// that pre-attaches a pool of workers and tears some of them down unused says
// so out loud, and `grep -r "sched:never-ran"` answers "did we allocate thread
// stacks we never scheduled". Its sibling `sched:unspawned` answers the same
// question one step earlier, for a thread that never reached the scheduler at
// all; it is also the recovery route out of a failed `attach`, and that dual
// role is stated rather than hidden.
//
// WHAT `run` DOES NOT ASSUME. <ran!> is a claim that the thread executed, so
// this module PROVES it rather than inferring it from a yield: the entry shim
// sets a flag as its first act, and `run` reads that flag after yielding. If
// the flag is clear the tor takes `| stalled` and hands the handle back in
// <queued!>. There is no arm that mints <ran!> without evidence.
//
// WHO OWNS A THREAD'S DEATH — the one design decision everything else follows
// from. Unikraft supports two disciplines and they are incompatible:
//
//   (A) OWNED. The body never exits by itself; the parent reaps it with
//       `uk_sched_thread_terminate`, which removes it from the scheduler, runs
//       the termination table and releases the memory in one call.
//   (B) SELF-EXITING. The body calls `uk_sched_thread_exit()`, the thread lands
//       on `sched->exited_threads`, and `uk_sched_thread_gc` releases it later.
//
// An obligation can only describe (A). Under (B) the parent's handle becomes a
// dangling pointer at a moment the parent cannot observe — `uk_sched_thread_remove`
// has already NULLed `t->sched` — so `uk_sched_thread_terminate` on it would trip
// its own `UK_ASSERT(thread->sched)` in a debug build and corrupt a freed
// allocation in a release one. **This lift models (A) only**, and it does not
// merely document that: the entry shim it owns cannot take route (B), because
// after the body's last lap it PARKS (`uk_thread_block` + yield, forever) instead
// of exiting. So `uk_sched_thread_exit`, `uk_sched_thread_exit2` and
// `uk_sched_thread_gc` are not bound here, and README.md says why rather than
// listing them as future work.
//
// WHAT A CALLER SUPPLIES, AND WHAT IT CANNOT. The C thread entry is a function
// pointer, `void (*)(void *) __noreturn`. **Koru has no first-class function
// value at the surface** — nothing in the language's test suite passes a flow or
// a tor as an argument — and "no threads at the surface" is a stated tenet of
// the language, so there is no spelling to invent here either. This module
// therefore OWNS the entry point and the caller supplies DATA: a thread name and
// a lap count. The shipped body announces each lap on the console and yields
// between laps, which is enough to drive every state in the machine against the
// real cooperative scheduler and to make the interleave visible. It is not a
// general-purpose "run my code on a thread" facility and README.md says so in
// those words, under claims not made.
//
// WHAT IS NOT RATCHETED. `census` walks the scheduler's thread list and takes
// and mints nothing: knowing how many threads exist constrains nothing about
// what you may do next. There is no obligation on `*Sched` either — the
// scheduler is created by `ukboot` before `main` and is never given back, so an
// obligation on it could not be discharged; the proof that one exists rides on
// being inside `current`'s `| ready` arm, exactly as `unikraft/pages` does with
// its `*Pager`.

// ============================================================================
// UNIKRAFT C ABI
// ============================================================================
//
// Every symbol below is on `lib/uksched/exportsyms.uk` (case 1 of the brief's
// three-case rule — the file exists, 42 lines, and objcopy localizes everything
// not on it) except `_uk_alloc_head`, which is on `lib/ukalloc/exportsyms.uk:22`.

const UkThread = opaque {};
const UkAlloc = opaque {};

// exportsyms.uk:22 of ukalloc. `uk_alloc_get_default()` is `static inline`
// (alloc.h:136) and returns this variable; reading it is the same load.
extern var _uk_alloc_head: ?*UkAlloc;

// thread.h:97, and uksched/exportsyms.uk:39. Declared here so the assembler in
// `currentThread` has a symbol to relocate against; it is never read directly —
// see that function for why.
extern var __uk_sched_thread_current: ?*ThreadRaw;

extern fn uk_thread_create_container(
    a: ?*UkAlloc,
    a_stack: ?*UkAlloc,
    stack_len: usize,
    a_auxstack: ?*UkAlloc,
    auxstack_len: usize,
    a_uktls: ?*UkAlloc,
    no_ectx: bool,
    name: ?[*:0]const u8,
    priv: ?*anyopaque,
    dtor: ?*const fn (*UkThread) callconv(.c) void,
) ?*UkThread;

extern fn uk_thread_container_init_fn1(
    t: *UkThread,
    fn1: *const fn (?*anyopaque) callconv(.c) noreturn,
    argp: ?*anyopaque,
) void;

extern fn uk_sched_thread_add(s: *SchedRaw, t: *UkThread) c_int;
extern fn uk_sched_thread_terminate(t: *UkThread) void;
extern fn uk_thread_release(t: *UkThread) void;
extern fn uk_thread_block(t: *UkThread) void;
extern fn uk_thread_wake(t: *UkThread) void;

// `sched_yield` is a syscall definition (sched.c:396) and is on the allowlist,
// so the plain C name links. It returns 0 unconditionally.
extern fn sched_yield() c_int;

// nolibc, in the same address space. No syscall shim, no libc emulation.
extern fn malloc(size: usize) ?*anyopaque;
extern fn free(ptr: ?*anyopaque) void;
extern fn printf(fmt: [*:0]const u8, ...) c_int;

// --- struct uk_sched, mirrored from lib/uksched/include/uk/sched.h:88.
//
// There is no exported accessor for ANY of these fields and no exported way to
// obtain a `struct uk_sched *` at all: `uk_sched_current()` is `static inline`
// (sched.h:53) over `uk_thread_current()`, which is also `static inline`
// (thread.h:100). So the scheduler pointer is reachable only through the layout
// below. That is the brief's case 3, and this module answers it the way
// `unikraft/pages` does: mirror, then PROVE the mirror at run time before using
// it. See `current`.
//
// Nothing in this declaration is conditional. The only `#ifdef` in the C struct
// is `CONFIG_LIBUKSCHED_STATS`, and it appends `start_time` and `stats` AFTER
// `next` — past everything mirrored here — so no build option can shift these
// offsets. (Contrast `unikraft/pages`, where `CONFIG_LIBUKALLOC_IFMALLOC`
// inserts two pointers mid-struct and needs a detector.)

const TailqHead = extern struct {
    first: ?*ThreadRaw,
    last: ?*anyopaque,
};

const TailqEntry = extern struct {
    next: ?*ThreadRaw,
    prev: ?*anyopaque,
};

const SchedRaw = extern struct {
    yield: ?*anyopaque, // 0
    thread_add: ?*anyopaque, // 8
    thread_remove: ?*anyopaque, // 16
    thread_blocked: ?*anyopaque, // 24
    thread_woken: ?*anyopaque, // 32
    thread_woken_isr: ?*anyopaque, // 40
    idle_thread: ?*anyopaque, // 48
    sched_start: ?*anyopaque, // 56
    is_started: u8, // 64
    _pad: [7]u8,
    thread_list: TailqHead, // 72
    exited_threads: TailqHead, // 88
    a: ?*UkAlloc, // 104
    a_stack: ?*UkAlloc, // 112
    a_auxstack: ?*UkAlloc, // 120
    a_uktls: ?*UkAlloc, // 128
    next: ?*SchedRaw, // 136
};

// --- struct uk_thread, thread.h:56. Only three fields are read: `sched` (to
// find the scheduler), `flags` (to name what state the C thinks it is in when a
// proof fails) and `thread_list` (to walk the scheduler's roster). The gaps are
// named by size rather than mirrored field by field, because a field this module
// never touches is a claim it does not need to make.

const ThreadRaw = extern struct {
    // ctx(16) ectx(8) tlsp(8) uktlsp(8) auxsp(8) queue(16) = 64
    _head: [64]u8,
    flags: u32, // 64
    _pad0: u32,
    wakeup_time: i64, // 72
    sched: ?*SchedRaw, // 80
    // wait_ticket(32) _mem(56) _gc_fn(8) _gc_argp(8) dtor(8) priv(8) exec_time(8) name(8)
    _mid: [136]u8, // 88
    thread_list: TailqEntry, // 224
};

// Measured against a built kernel — Unikraft 0.21.0 "Ijiraq", x86_64, the
// version `Kraftfile: unikraft.version: stable` fetches. The measurement is a
// `printf("%zu", offsetof(...))` in a probe image, reproduced in README.md so
// the numbers can be re-derived rather than believed. These assertions prove the
// mirror is SELF-CONSISTENT; `current`'s run-time proof is what checks it against
// the kernel actually linked in.
comptime {
    if (@offsetOf(SchedRaw, "sched_start") != 56) @compileError("uk_sched.sched_start moved");
    if (@offsetOf(SchedRaw, "is_started") != 64) @compileError("uk_sched.is_started moved");
    if (@offsetOf(SchedRaw, "thread_list") != 72) @compileError("uk_sched.thread_list moved");
    if (@offsetOf(SchedRaw, "exited_threads") != 88) @compileError("uk_sched.exited_threads moved");
    if (@offsetOf(SchedRaw, "a") != 104) @compileError("uk_sched.a moved");
    if (@offsetOf(SchedRaw, "a_stack") != 112) @compileError("uk_sched.a_stack moved");
    if (@offsetOf(SchedRaw, "a_auxstack") != 120) @compileError("uk_sched.a_auxstack moved");
    if (@offsetOf(SchedRaw, "a_uktls") != 128) @compileError("uk_sched.a_uktls moved");
    if (@offsetOf(SchedRaw, "next") != 136) @compileError("uk_sched.next moved");
    if (@sizeOf(SchedRaw) != 144) @compileError("uk_sched changed size");

    if (@offsetOf(ThreadRaw, "flags") != 64) @compileError("uk_thread.flags moved");
    if (@offsetOf(ThreadRaw, "wakeup_time") != 72) @compileError("uk_thread.wakeup_time moved");
    if (@offsetOf(ThreadRaw, "sched") != 80) @compileError("uk_thread.sched moved");
    if (@offsetOf(ThreadRaw, "thread_list") != 224) @compileError("uk_thread.thread_list moved");
    if (@sizeOf(ThreadRaw) != 240) @compileError("uk_thread changed size");
}

// UK_THREADF_*, thread.h:163-175.
const F_RUNNABLE: u32 = 0x008;
const F_EXITED: u32 = 0x010;

// ============================================================================
// THE HANDLES
// ============================================================================

// The proven scheduler. A caller never sees `struct uk_sched *`.
const Sched = extern struct {
    s: *SchedRaw,
};

var scheduler: Sched = undefined;

// One thread. `t` is the C thread; everything else is this lift's own
// bookkeeping, allocated beside it.
//
// `started` and `finished` are written by the worker and read by the parent.
// They are the evidence behind <ran!>: without them `run` would be asserting
// that a yield scheduled the thread, which is a claim about the scheduler's
// policy and not an observation.
const Thread = extern struct {
    t: ?*UkThread,
    name: ?[*:0]u8,
    laps: u32,
    lap: u32,
    started: u32,
    finished: u32,
};

// The current thread, read exactly the way `uk_thread_current()` reads it.
//
// `__uk_sched_thread_current` is `__uk_pcpuvar` (sched.c:50): the symbol
// addresses a TEMPLATE in `.uk_pcpuvar`, and each CPU's live copy sits at
// `template + tmpl_size * cpu_idx`, reached through the GS base. On x86_64 that
// is one `mov %gs:sym(%rip)` — lib/ukpcpuvar/arch/x86_64/include/uk/pcpuvar/arch.h,
// `__uk_pcpuvar_arch_current_get`. This is that instruction, so the read is
// correct on any CPU.
//
// A plain load of the extern variable happens to give the same answer on the
// boot CPU (measured: cpu_idx 0, so the live copy IS the template) and would be
// a silent lie on any other. The instruction costs the same; the assumption is
// what would have cost something.
// Both architectures Unikraft's `ukpcpuvar` implements are spelled out, and
// anything else is a COMPILE ERROR naming the gap. There is no portable
// fallback here and there must not be: a plain load of the extern variable is
// the wrong instruction on every CPU but the boot one, and it fails silently —
// it returns the template's copy, which on CPU 0 is right by coincidence.
const uk_arch = @import("builtin").cpu.arch;

// The arm64 form needs the per-CPU section base, which the linker script
// defines (`lib/ukpcpuvar/include/uk/pcpuvar.h:20`). It is never dereferenced,
// only subtracted.
extern const _uk_pcpuvar_base: anyopaque;

fn currentThread() ?*ThreadRaw {
    return switch (uk_arch) {
        .x86_64 => asm volatile ("mov %%gs:__uk_sched_thread_current(%%rip), %[ret]"
            : [ret] "=r" (-> ?*ThreadRaw),
            :
            : .{ .memory = true }),
        .aarch64 => blk: {
            const off = @intFromPtr(&__uk_sched_thread_current) - @intFromPtr(&_uk_pcpuvar_base);
            break :blk asm volatile (
                \\mrs %[ret], tpidr_el1
                \\ldr %[ret], [%[ret], %[off]]
                : [ret] "=&r" (-> ?*ThreadRaw),
                : [off] "r" (off),
                : .{ .memory = true });
        },
        else => @compileError("unikraft/sched: uk_thread_current() reads a per-CPU variable and ukpcpuvar implements only x86_64 (GS-relative) and arm64 (TPIDR_EL1); this target has neither"),
    };
}

// Walk `s`'s roster, stopping at `stop` if it is found. Returns the number of
// threads seen and whether `stop` was among them.
//
// `while`, not `for`: a linked-list walk has no trip count known before it
// starts. The bound is a refusal to loop forever on a corrupt list, not a count.
const ROSTER_BOUND: u32 = 1024;

fn rosterWalk(s: *SchedRaw, stop: ?*ThreadRaw) struct { n: u32, found: bool, overran: bool } {
    var n: u32 = 0;
    var found = false;
    var cur = s.thread_list.first;
    while (cur) |t| {
        if (stop != null and t == stop.?) found = true;
        n += 1;
        if (n > ROSTER_BOUND) return .{ .n = n, .found = found, .overran = true };
        cur = t.thread_list.next;
    }
    return .{ .n = n, .found = found, .overran = false };
}

fn destroy(h: *Thread) void {
    if (h.name) |p| free(@ptrCast(p));
    free(@ptrCast(h));
}

// Copy a Koru `string` into a NUL-terminated allocation the C can keep.
// `uk_thread_create_container` stores the pointer and `uk_sched_dumpk_threads`
// prints it, so it must outlive the call — a borrow of the caller's bytes would
// be a dangling `name` field the moment the flow moved on.
fn dupz(text: []const u8) ?[*:0]u8 {
    const raw = malloc(text.len + 1) orelse return null;
    const p: [*]u8 = @ptrCast(raw);
    for (0..text.len) |i| p[i] = text[i];
    p[text.len] = 0;
    return @ptrCast(p);
}

// ============================================================================
// THE ENTRY POINT THIS MODULE OWNS
// ============================================================================

// The one thread body in this lift. See the module header for why the caller
// supplies data and not code.
//
// It does three things, in this order, and the order is the whole contract:
//
//   1. Raises `started` as its FIRST act, so `run`'s proof is an observation of
//      the thread having executed at least one instruction.
//   2. Runs `laps` laps, announcing each and yielding after it. The yield is
//      what makes the interleave with the parent real and visible, rather than
//      the parent seeing one uninterrupted burst.
//   3. PARKS FOREVER instead of returning. `uk_thread_fn1_t` is `__noreturn` and
//      the two legal ways to leave are `uk_sched_thread_exit()` — which puts the
//      thread on the garbage-collection list and invalidates the parent's handle
//      at a moment the parent cannot see — or never leaving. This body never
//      leaves. That is what makes the parent's obligation honest: while this
//      loop is running, `terminate` is always a legal call and always describes
//      a thread that is still there.
fn workerBody(argp: ?*anyopaque) callconv(.c) noreturn {
    const h: *Thread = @ptrCast(@alignCast(argp.?));
    @atomicStore(u32, &h.started, 1, .release);

    // `h.laps` is read inline rather than hoisted into a local. A host-line
    // `const laps` ANYWHERE in this module — even function-local, even here —
    // makes koruc stop binding the `laps` input of `prime` to a local and fall
    // back to unscoped textual substitution of the token, which then rewrites
    // `thread.laps` into `thread.__koru_event_input.laps` and edits the text
    // inside string literals. Minimal repro and the emitted evidence are in
    // README.md, "What the toolchain got wrong". Nothing about the lift's design
    // wants a local here; the comment exists so nobody re-adds one.
    for (0..h.laps) |i| {
        const n: u32 = @intCast(i + 1);
        @atomicStore(u32, &h.lap, n, .release);
        _ = printf("  [%s] lap %u of %u, on its own stack\n", h.name.?, @as(c_uint, n), @as(c_uint, h.laps));
        _ = sched_yield();
    }

    @atomicStore(u32, &h.finished, 1, .release);

    // No trip count: this loop ends when the parent reaps the thread, and the
    // parent's call never returns here.
    while (true) {
        uk_thread_block(h.t.?);
        _ = sched_yield();
    }
}

// ============================================================================
// ACQUIRE THE SCHEDULER
// ============================================================================

// The running scheduler, PROVEN, or an explicit refusal naming which proof
// failed.
//
// A mirrored struct is an ABI CLAIM, and an ABI claim that is wrong corrupts
// memory silently. `unikraft/blk` guards its mirror with `@offsetOf` assertions,
// which prove the mirror is self-consistent and say nothing about the C.
// `unikraft/pages` interrogates the C at run time by comparing table slots
// against exported symbol addresses. Neither technique is available here:
// `struct uk_sched`'s function pointers are FILE STATICS in `lib/ukschedcoop`
// (`schedcoop_yield` and friends are not on any exportsyms.uk), so this archive
// cannot know a single one of their addresses.
//
// So the proof is STRUCTURAL instead — it checks an invariant the scheduler
// maintains rather than an address it holds. Six tests, all read-only, no call
// made through any pointer until every one has passed:
//
//   1. `uk_thread_current()` is non-NULL. Before `uk_sched_start` it is NULL
//      (sched.c:213 asserts exactly that), so this also proves boot got far
//      enough.
//   2. `t->sched` at offset 80 is non-NULL and word-aligned.
//   3. `s->is_started` is true — sched.c:249 sets it, and nothing clears it.
//   4. `s->next` is NULL and `s->a` and `s->a_uktls` are `_uk_alloc_head`, the
//      one allocator address this archive knows independently
//      (`ukboot/boot.c:360` passes `uk_alloc_get_default()` as both). `a_stack`
//      and `a_auxstack` are checked non-NULL only: `ukboot` hands those two
//      separate stack allocators, which the probe confirmed.
//   5. Every vtable slot the scheduler must have — yield, thread_add,
//      thread_remove, thread_blocked, thread_woken, sched_start — is non-NULL.
//      `uk_sched_start` asserts `sched_start` (sched.c:211) and
//      `uk_sched_thread_add` calls `thread_add` unconditionally.
//   6. THE ROUND TRIP, and the test that makes the other five more than a
//      plausibility argument: walk `s->thread_list` and find the current thread
//      on it. That list is threaded through `uk_thread.thread_list` at offset
//      224, and membership is the exact invariant `uk_sched_thread_add`
//      (sched.c:373) and `uk_sched_start` (sched.c:241) maintain. Passing it
//      proves offset 80 really is `sched`, offset 72 really is that scheduler's
//      roster, and offset 224 really is a thread's link on it — three mirrored
//      offsets confirmed by one walk, in a structure that could not close by
//      accident.
//
// If any test fails, `current` takes `| unavailable` and names it. Because
// `current` is the only source of a `*Sched` and `reserve` is the only source of
// a `*Thread`, nothing in this module is reachable after a refusal. There is no
// degraded path and no assumed layout.
//
// Cost: about a dozen loads plus one walk of a list that holds two threads at
// boot. A program calls `current` once and threads the `*Sched`.
~pub tor current { }
| ready *Sched
| unavailable string

~proc current|zig {
    const t = currentThread() orelse {
        return .{ .unavailable = "uk_thread_current() is NULL: uk_sched_start has not run, so there is no scheduler to attach threads to" };
    };

    const s = t.sched orelse {
        return .{ .unavailable = "the current thread is not attached to a scheduler" };
    };
    if ((@intFromPtr(s) & 0x7) != 0) {
        return .{ .unavailable = "the word at uk_thread+80 is not an aligned pointer, so it is not struct uk_sched * and unikraft/sched refuses to guess the layout" };
    }

    if (s.is_started == 0) {
        return .{ .unavailable = "struct uk_sched.is_started reads false while a thread is running on it, which uk_sched_start never produces: the mirrored layout is not this kernel's" };
    }
    if (s.next != null) {
        return .{ .unavailable = "struct uk_sched.next is non-NULL: more than one scheduler is registered, and this lift models the single scheduler ukboot starts" };
    }
    if (s.a != _uk_alloc_head or s.a_uktls != _uk_alloc_head) {
        return .{ .unavailable = "struct uk_sched.a / .a_uktls are not the default allocator ukboot passed to uk_schedcoop_create, so the mirrored layout is not this kernel's" };
    }
    if (s.a_stack == null or s.a_auxstack == null) {
        return .{ .unavailable = "struct uk_sched has no stack or aux-stack allocator, so no thread container can be built" };
    }
    if (s.yield == null or s.thread_add == null or s.thread_remove == null or
        s.thread_blocked == null or s.thread_woken == null or s.sched_start == null)
    {
        return .{ .unavailable = "struct uk_sched's callback table has a NULL slot, which uk_sched_init never produces: the mirrored layout is not this kernel's" };
    }

    const walk = rosterWalk(s, t);
    if (walk.overran) {
        return .{ .unavailable = "the scheduler's thread list did not terminate within 1024 entries, so offset 72 or offset 224 is not what this lift mirrors" };
    }
    if (!walk.found) {
        return .{ .unavailable = "the current thread is not on its own scheduler's thread list: the mirrored offsets do not describe this kernel and unikraft/sched refuses to write through them" };
    }

    scheduler = Sched{ .s = s };
    return .{ .ready = &scheduler };
}

// How many threads the scheduler is holding, counted by walking the same list
// `current`'s proof walked. At boot that is two — `init` and `idle` — and it
// goes up by one per `attach` and down by one per `terminate`.
//
// No obligation: knowing the roster size constrains nothing about what you may
// do next. This is the module's `blk:count`.
//
// There is no exported way to ask. `uk_sched_dumpk_threads` prints the roster to
// the kernel log and returns nothing, and `uk_sched_foreach_thread` is a macro.
~pub tor census { sched: *Sched } -> u32

~proc census|zig {
    return rosterWalk(sched.s, null).n;
}

// ============================================================================
// BUILD A THREAD
// ============================================================================

// `uk_thread_create_container`: a thread struct with a stack, an aux stack, a
// TLS and extended-context space, but NO entry point and NOT runnable.
//
// The allocators are not the caller's problem. `uk_thread_create_container`
// takes four of them and getting them wrong is how you allocate a thread stack
// out of the TLS pool; they are read from the proven `*Sched`, which is exactly
// what `uk_sched_thread_create_fn1` (sched.c:114) does internally.
//
// `stack` is the stack size in bytes; 0 means the kernel default
// (`__STACK_SIZE`, which `CONFIG_STACK_SIZE_PAGE_ORDER` sets — order 6, 256 KiB,
// in every image this module's tests build).
//
// Mints <container!>. The only tors that accept it are `prime` and the named
// escape `unspawned`: there is deliberately no path from a fresh container to
// `terminate`.
~pub tor reserve { sched: *Sched, name: string, stack: u64 }
| thread *Thread<container!>
| refused string

~proc reserve|zig {
    if (name.len == 0) {
        return .{ .refused = "a thread needs a name: uk_sched_dumpk_threads prints it and an unnamed thread is unidentifiable in a crash dump" };
    }

    const mem = malloc(@sizeOf(Thread)) orelse {
        return .{ .refused = "out of memory allocating the thread handle" };
    };
    const h: *Thread = @ptrCast(@alignCast(mem));
    h.* = Thread{ .t = null, .name = null, .laps = 0, .lap = 0, .started = 0, .finished = 0 };

    h.name = dupz(name) orelse {
        free(mem);
        return .{ .refused = "out of memory copying the thread name" };
    };

    const s = sched.s;
    const t = uk_thread_create_container(
        s.a,
        s.a_stack,
        @intCast(stack),
        s.a_auxstack,
        0,
        s.a_uktls,
        false,
        h.name,
        null,
        null,
    ) orelse {
        destroy(h);
        return .{ .refused = "uk_thread_create_container could not allocate the thread, its stack, its aux stack or its TLS" };
    };

    h.t = t;
    return .{ .thread = h };
}

// `uk_thread_container_init_fn1`: install the entry point and mark the thread
// runnable.
//
// THIS IS THE TOR THAT LIFTS THE CONTAINER ASSERTIONS. thread.c:872-873 checks
// `t->ctx.ip == 0x0` and `!(t->flags & UK_THREADF_RUNNABLE)` — together, "the
// thing you handed me is a container nobody has primed yet". Priming a second
// time overwrites the instruction pointer and the stack pointer of a thread that
// may be mid-execution; with asserts off, the C does it without a word. Here
// `prime` consumes <!container> and mints <primed!>, and nothing mints
// <container!> except `reserve`, so a second `prime` has nothing to consume.
//
// `laps` is how many times the body announces itself before parking. It is data,
// not code — see the module header on what a caller can and cannot supply.
//
// The failure arm hands the handle back in <container!>: nothing was installed,
// so the thread is exactly what `reserve` produced and `prime` is still the
// correct next call.
~pub tor prime { thread: *Thread<!container>, laps: u32 }
| ok *Thread<primed!>
| rejected { thread: *Thread<container!>, reason: string }

~proc prime|zig {
    if (laps == 0) {
        return .{ .rejected = .{ .thread = thread, .reason = "a thread with zero laps would park without ever announcing itself, which is indistinguishable from a thread that never ran" } };
    }
    thread.laps = laps;
    uk_thread_container_init_fn1(thread.t.?, &workerBody, @ptrCast(thread));
    return .{ .ok = thread };
}

// `uk_sched_thread_add`: hand the primed thread to the scheduler.
//
// Lifts sched.c:364, `UK_ASSERT(!t->sched)` — "this thread is not already
// attached". <!primed> is minted only by `prime` and consumed here, so a second
// `attach` has nothing to consume and an `attach` of a bare container cannot be
// spelled. It also lifts `schedcoop_thread_add`'s
// `UK_ASSERT(!uk_thread_is_exited(t))` (schedcoop.c): in this module an exited
// thread has no handle at all, because `terminate` and `release` both consume
// theirs.
//
// A failure hands back <primed!> — the scheduler refused it, so it is exactly
// what `prime` produced, and `unspawned` is the way out.
~pub tor attach { thread: *Thread<!primed> }
| ok *Thread<queued!>
| refused { thread: *Thread<primed!>, errno: i32, reason: string }

~proc attach|zig {
    const rc = uk_sched_thread_add(scheduler.s, thread.t.?);
    if (rc < 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .thread = thread, .errno = @intCast(rc), .reason = "uk_sched_thread_add refused the thread" } };
    }
    return .{ .ok = thread };
}

// ============================================================================
// GIVE IT CPU
// ============================================================================

// Yield the current thread once, then check that the attached thread actually
// executed.
//
// `sched_yield` hands the CPU to the next runnable thread; under `ukschedcoop`
// that is round-robin over the run queue, so a freshly attached thread is
// reached within one pass. The pass length is not assumed: it is the roster
// size, which `current` already proved this module can count.
//
// Mints <ran!> ONLY on evidence. The body raises `started` as its first act; if
// the flag is still clear after a full pass the tor takes `| stalled` and hands
// the handle back in <queued!> — the weaker of the two claims, and the honest
// one, at the cost of spending a `ran` that a re-entrant call had earned. That
// cost is the same one `unikraft/blk` states for its `rejected` arm and it is
// preferred to a hole in the gate.
~pub tor run { thread: *Thread<!queued|!ran> }
| ok *Thread<ran!>
| stalled { thread: *Thread<queued!>, reason: string }

~proc run|zig {
    const pass = rosterWalk(scheduler.s, null).n;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i <= pass) : (i += 1) {
        if (@atomicLoad(u32, &thread.started, .acquire) != 0) {
            return .{ .ok = thread };
        }
        _ = sched_yield();
    }
    if (@atomicLoad(u32, &thread.started, .acquire) != 0) {
        return .{ .ok = thread };
    }
    return .{ .stalled = .{ .thread = thread, .reason = "the thread did not execute within one full pass of the run queue" } };
}

// Yield until the thread's body has finished all its laps.
//
// Unikraft has no join. There is no `uk_sched_thread_join` on `exportsyms.uk`
// and none in the headers: a parent that wants to know its child is done either
// shares a flag or uses a wait queue, and both are things it writes itself. This
// tor is the flag, owned by the lift, so a caller does not write it again — and
// `laps` comes back as evidence of what was actually run.
//
// The wait after the first pass is UNBOUNDED, exactly as `unikraft/blk`'s
// transfer wait is: a body that never finishes is a hung program either way, and
// a timeout would be a policy the C does not have and an arm that lies about
// what happened. The bounded part is only the start check, which is what
// distinguishes "still working" from "never scheduled".
~pub tor join { thread: *Thread<!queued|!ran> }
| done { thread: *Thread<ran!>, laps: u64 }
| stalled { thread: *Thread<queued!>, reason: string }

~proc join|zig {
    const pass = rosterWalk(scheduler.s, null).n;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i <= pass and @atomicLoad(u32, &thread.started, .acquire) == 0) : (i += 1) {
        _ = sched_yield();
    }
    if (@atomicLoad(u32, &thread.started, .acquire) == 0) {
        return .{ .stalled = .{ .thread = thread, .reason = "the thread did not execute within one full pass of the run queue" } };
    }

    while (@atomicLoad(u32, &thread.finished, .acquire) == 0) {
        _ = sched_yield();
    }
    return .{ .done = .{ .thread = thread, .laps = @intCast(@atomicLoad(u32, &thread.lap, .acquire)) } };
}

// `uk_thread_block`: take the thread out of the run queue.
//
// Lifts sched.h:138, `UK_ASSERT(!uk_thread_is_runnable(t))` inside
// `uk_sched_thread_blocked` — the scheduler callback the exported
// `uk_thread_block` reaches. Blocking a thread twice is what that assertion is
// there to catch, and here <parked!> is consumed by `unpark` and by nothing
// else, so a second `park` has nothing to consume.
//
// No failure arm: `uk_thread_block` returns void and cannot fail.
~pub tor park { thread: *Thread<!ran> } -> *Thread<parked!>

~proc park|zig {
    uk_thread_block(thread.t.?);
    return thread;
}

// `uk_thread_wake`: put the thread back in the run queue.
//
// Lifts sched.h:151, `UK_ASSERT(uk_thread_is_runnable(t))` inside
// `uk_sched_thread_woken`. Mints <ran!> and not <queued!> because a thread that
// can be parked has already been given CPU — the state is a memory of what
// happened, and waking cannot un-run it.
//
// No failure arm: `uk_thread_wake` returns void.
~pub tor unpark { thread: *Thread<!parked> } -> *Thread<ran!>

~proc unpark|zig {
    uk_thread_wake(thread.t.?);
    return thread;
}

// THE NAMED ESCAPE from the asymmetry gate. `terminate` accepts only <!ran> or
// <!parked>, and only actually running the thread mints <ran!> — so a worker
// attached and torn down without ever being scheduled cannot be spelled. That is
// the point; this is how a program that means it says so.
//
// Deliberately greppable: `grep -r "sched:never-ran"` answers "which programs
// allocate thread stacks they never schedule". It runs no C call, which is the
// honest emission — nothing happened.
~pub tor never-ran { thread: *Thread<!queued> } -> *Thread<ran!>

~proc never-ran|zig {
    return thread;
}

// ============================================================================
// TEAR DOWN
// ============================================================================

// `uk_sched_thread_terminate`: remove from the scheduler, run the termination
// table, and release the thread's memory — one call, because that is what the C
// does for a thread that is not the caller (sched.c:327).
//
// Lifts sched.c:300, `UK_ASSERT(thread->sched)`, whose own comment says it "can
// also fail on a double-termination". Here the handle is CONSUMED, so a second
// `terminate` has nothing to pass and the double-termination the C can only
// notice with asserts on is not a program that exists. It also lifts
// thread.c:1004, `UK_ASSERT(t != uk_thread_current())`: a `*Thread` in this
// module is always a thread the caller created and never the caller itself,
// because `current` hands out a `*Sched` and no handle on the running thread.
//
// No failure arm: `uk_sched_thread_terminate` returns void.
~pub tor terminate { thread: *Thread<!ran|!parked> }

~proc terminate|zig {
    uk_sched_thread_terminate(thread.t.?);
    destroy(thread);
}

// The way out for a thread that never reached the scheduler — a container that
// was not primed, a primed thread the scheduler refused, or a worker a program
// decided not to start after all.
//
// Two roles in one tor, said out loud rather than split: it is the named escape
// at the level below `never-ran`, AND it is the recovery arm out of a failed
// `attach`. Splitting them would give two tors with identical bodies and
// identical states, which is ceremony, not information.
//
// Runs no C call: an unattached container needs nothing undone before
// `uk_thread_release`, which asserts precisely `!t->sched` (thread.c:1005).
~pub tor unspawned { thread: *Thread<!container|!primed> } -> *Thread<detached!>

~proc unspawned|zig {
    return thread;
}

// `uk_thread_release`: run the destructor and free the thread, its stack, its
// aux stack and its TLS.
//
// Lifts thread.c:1005, `UK_ASSERT(!t->sched)` — "thread must be disconnected
// from scheduler". <!detached> is minted only by `unspawned`, which is reachable
// only from <container> or <primed>, and both of those are states in which the
// thread has never been attached. So releasing a live scheduler thread is
// unspellable, and the assertion has nothing left to catch.
//
// No failure arm: `uk_thread_release` returns void.
~pub tor release { thread: *Thread<!detached> }

~proc release|zig {
    uk_thread_release(thread.t.?);
    destroy(thread);
}
